# What gets published to the web, and what does not.
#
# Every top-level entry in the repository must appear here. `tools/publish.py` refuses to
# run if something is unclassified, so a new directory cannot be silently served — or
# silently left out of the deploy, which is the failure that is harder to notice.
#
# The bias is towards serving source: the argument this project makes is that the code
# computing the numbers is the code your browser ran, and that is only true if the modules
# are actually there to fetch. What stays back is everything with no reason to be on a
# public hostname — test harnesses, build tooling, the data pipeline, the docs GitHub
# already renders.
#
#   served    copied into the deployed tree
#   excluded  stays in the repository only

served    index.html          the fleet monitor
served    concept             the how-it-works page
served    ship                the public 3D viewer: the ship, joint by joint, and its live model
served    engineering         the public engineering story: seven scales, live numbers, honest walls
served    cell                the gated working pages: flat explainer, blueprint, checks, band calculator
served    model-lab           the 3D development lab (noindex; linked from the 3D README)
served    float               the float case, its ledger and the member census: three pages rendered from docs/
served    sim                 the model — the whole point of publishing source
served    app                 the monitor application
served    data                terrain, water, outline, offline snapshots
served    data/live/README.md server-owned mirror contract; feed JSON remains excluded
served    media               vehicle renders used by the pages

# 3d/ is served, but not all of it: see PARTIAL below.
served    3d                  the WebGL vehicle library

excluded  docs                prose; GitHub renders it, the web server need not
excluded  research            evidence store and the reports written from it — GitHub renders it
excluded  tests               harnesses and golden files
excluded  tools               development tooling, including the migration scripts
excluded  pipeline            the Python data generators; they run on a server, not in a page
excluded  .github             CI and templates
excluded  .git                —
excluded  .gitignore          —
excluded  dist.manifest       —
excluded  Makefile            —
excluded  requirements.txt    —
excluded  README.md           —
excluded  CITATION.cff        citation metadata remains in the public source repository/GitHub, not the deployed site
excluded  GOALS.md            what the project is working toward, and in what proportion
excluded  CONTRIBUTING.md     —
excluded  CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md  —
excluded  SECURITY.md         —
served    LICENSE             Apache-2.0 licence for the source code
served    LICENSE-CONTENT     CC BY 4.0 licence for our own text and figures
served    NOTICE              redistribution notices for third-party material
served    DATA-SOURCES.md     source, transformation and licence details for bundled data
served    notices.html        generated data, licences and notices page
excluded  CHANGELOG.md        —
excluded  .editorconfig       —
excluded  .claude             local agent tooling settings; machine-specific and never tracked
excluded  series              local integration patches and tree receipts
excluded  inputs              local audit inputs, not site content
excluded  shots               local visual evidence, not site content
excluded  HANDUP.md           local verification record, not site content
excluded  REMOVALS.md         local removal review, not site content

# Paths inside a served directory that stay behind anyway. A test page and a probe harness
# on a production hostname are an invitation, and the raster exports are regenerable bulk.
PARTIAL   3d/tests            test harness
PARTIAL   ship/_hero_test.html private hero test harness
PARTIAL   3d/scripts          build and probe scripts
PARTIAL   3d/assets/raster    regenerable exports
PARTIAL   3d/assets/renders   GPU captures of the vehicle. Nothing in the served tree imports
                              them: they are the source for the website's own media/ and for the
                              report PDFs, and both take a resized copy. Serving the originals
                              would put a megabyte of unreferenced PNG on the CDN.
PARTIAL   data/live           written on the server every ten minutes by pipeline/live.py;
                              publishing the repository's copy would push stale fire data
                              over a fresh mirror

excluded  .browser-scratch    disposable browser profiles and probes, including interrupted runs
excluded  .xo-evidence        local verification commands and outputs, not site content
excluded  .helm               generated paths the commit analysis skips; tooling, not site content
